   "stem": {
      "text": "The integral test is applied to the series \\( \\sum_{n=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\) using \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\). Take the lower limit of integration to be the series' starting index. Find the exact value of the improper integral of f from that lower limit to infinity."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         1,
         2
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Take \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\), so \\( f(n) \\) is the general term. On \\( [2, \\infty) \\), \\( f \\) is positive, continuous, and decreasing, so the integral test applies.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            1,
            [
               "Power",
               "x",
               2
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The series starts at \\( n = 2 \\), so the matching integral is \\( \\int_{2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\, dx = \\lim_{b \\to \\infty} \\int_{2}^{b} \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\, dx \\).",
         "mathjson": 2
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "An antiderivative is \\( F(x) = - \\frac{1}{x} \\), so \\( \\int_{2}^{b} \\frac{1}{x^{2}} \\, dx = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{b} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Rational",
               1,
               2
            ],
            [
               "Negate",
               [
                  "Divide",
                  1,
                  "b"
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "As \\( b \\to \\infty \\), \\( F(b) \\to 0 \\), so the integral converges to \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\). This number is the value of the integral, not the sum of the series.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            2
         ]
      }
   ],
